To repeat what I said on the other PR:

@jasonmolenda @JDevlieghere Since Adrian is away, please have a look!

I would be happy to drop this test to be honest, it was Claude-coded and had to be assembly in order to bypass the confluence of factors that expose this bug: optimizations (specialisations + inlining), and code layout.

RegisterContextUnwind does have
```
  /// How far into the function we've executed. 0 if no instructions have been
  /// executed yet, std::nullopt if unknown.
  std::optional<int> m_current_offset;

  // How far into the function we've executed. 0 if no instructions have been
  // executed yet, std::nullopt if unknown. On architectures where the return
  // address on the stack points to the instruction after the CALL, this value
  // will have 1 subtracted from it. Otherwise, a function that ends in a CALL
  // will have an offset pointing into the next function's address range.
  // m_current_pc has the actual address of the "current" pc.
  std::optional<int> m_current_offset_backed_up_one;
```

but none of this is exposed outside of the class today.

`mergeTypes` still has this snippet using old attributes only:
```
    // Merge two pointer types, while trying to preserve typedef info
    const PointerType *LHSPointer = LHS->castAs<PointerType>();
    const PointerType *RHSPointer = RHS->castAs<PointerType>();
    BoundsSafetyPointerAttributes LHSFA = LHSPointer->getPointerAttributes();
    BoundsSafetyPointerAttributes RHSFA = RHSPointer->getPointerAttributes();
    if (!BoundsSafetyPointerAttributes::areCompatible(LHSFA, RHSFA))
```

`PointerType::isSingle()` seems like footgun now and can probably be removed.
By adding:
```
private:
using Type::isSinglePointerType();
using Type::isUnsafeIndexablePointerType();
```
to `PointerType`, we should be able to prevent people from doing silly things like `Ty->getAs<PointerType>()->isSinglePointerType()` as well.

> `mergeTypes` still has this snippet using old attributes only:
> 
> ```
>     // Merge two pointer types, while trying to preserve typedef info
>     const PointerType *LHSPointer = LHS->castAs<PointerType>();
>     const PointerType *RHSPointer = RHS->castAs<PointerType>();
>     BoundsSafetyPointerAttributes LHSFA = LHSPointer->getPointerAttributes();
>     BoundsSafetyPointerAttributes RHSFA = RHSPointer->getPointerAttributes();
>     if (!BoundsSafetyPointerAttributes::areCompatible(LHSFA, RHSFA))
> ```

Fixed — now uses getSugarAwareBoundsSafetyPointerAttributes() + areEquivalentLayouts() instead of the raw PointerType attributes, so the new sugar attributes are accounted for here.

@ojhunt 

> I'm unsure about this -- is there a reason functions like bad_read are not marked noinline? because the root of the problem is that llvm knows the constant array bounds. That means it can also constant fold the soft checks - e.g. it might not actually be testing dynamic behavior

I think not using `__attribute__((noinline))` was just a mistake. I never added it because it was never necessary while I was bringing up the test suite. If that fixes the problem then to me this would be the right fix here.

Please test with this PR:
https://github.com/swiftlang/swift/pull/90109

@swift-ci Please test
